{"builder":"Make one pull request for issue https://github.com/o/settings/issues/31 that meets both acceptance lines:\n- loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\n- npm test passes\n\nContext: src/config.js currently does `return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));`. src/app.js wraps loadConfig in try/catch, prints `bad config: <message>` and exits 1; README.md documents that behaviour. test/config.test.js has a test 'bad JSON throws' that asserts a SyntaxError, which conflicts with the new behaviour.\n\nSteps:\n1. In src/config.js, keep reading the file with readFileSync outside the try (a missing or unreadable file should still throw as before; only the parse error is caught). Wrap only the JSON.parse call in try/catch. In the catch, return {}.\n2. The issue says people can't tell which file is broken or where, so the {} return must not be silent: in the catch, write one line to stderr that names the path and includes the parse error's message (Node's SyntaxError message carries the position), e.g. `process.stderr.write(`bad config in ${path}: ${error.message}\\n`)`. Do not rethrow.\n3. Update test/config.test.js: replace 'bad JSON throws' with a test that loadConfig on a file containing `{port: 9000` returns a value deepEqual to {} (and does not throw). Keep 'reads a config'. Optionally add a test that a missing file still throws.\n4. Leave src/app.js working: with {} returned it falls back to DEFAULTS. Its existing try/catch can stay for read errors. Update README.md so it no longer says invalid JSON stops the app with exit 1; say it prints a warning naming the file and the parse error and starts with the defaults.\n5. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure every test passes. Also run `node src/app.js <a file with bad JSON>` once to see the warning line on stderr and `listening on 8080` on stdout.\n6. Commit and open the pull request referencing issue #31.","verifier":"Run every check at the pull request's head, from the repository root. Install nothing beyond what the repository needs (it has no dependencies).\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRead src/config.js at the head to confirm loadConfig is exported from it and catches the error from JSON.parse. Then run this single command:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import {mkdtempSync,writeFileSync} from 'node:fs'; import {tmpdir} from 'node:os'; import {join} from 'node:path'; import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; const f=join(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(),'cfg-')),'config.json'); writeFileSync(f,'{port: 9000'); let r; try { r=loadConfig(f); } catch(e) { console.log('THREW', e && e.name); process.exit(0); } console.log('RESULT', JSON.stringify(r), Object.getPrototypeOf(r)===Object.prototype && Object.keys(r).length===0 ? 'EMPTY_OBJECT' : 'NOT_EMPTY');\"\nPASS: the call on the invalid-JSON file does not throw and returns an empty plain object {} (output shows `RESULT {} EMPTY_OBJECT`), and loadConfig is in src/config.js.\nFAIL: loadConfig throws on the invalid JSON (output starts with THREW), returns anything other than {}, or loadConfig is not in src/config.js.\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test\nPASS: the test run reports no failing tests (node --test summary shows fail 0).\nFAIL: any test fails or the test run errors out.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions):\n- The issue body says people need to know which file is broken and where. Note whether the catch writes a stderr line naming the path and the parse error's message; e.g. run `node src/app.js <bad-json-file>` and look at stderr. A silent {} meets the line's words but leaves the issue's need unmet; report it if so.\n- Note whether only the JSON.parse error is caught (a missing file should still throw), whether the old 'bad JSON throws' test was replaced by one asserting {}, and whether README.md was updated to stop promising exit 1 on invalid JSON.\n- Note npm test's exit status (expected 0)."}